# campanile — a free-standing renaissance bell tower, 34.65 m to the tip of the vane.
#
# A Lombard-Venetian campanile: a plain brick-and-stone shaft on a two-stepped granite plinth,
# lesene strips and blind-arcaded corbel tables at every stage, slit windows deeply splayed, a
# spiral stair of a hundred and thirty-five risers inside it, a belfry of eight round-headed
# biforate openings on marble colonnettes with a bronze bell hung in the middle, a clock, a copper
# pyramid roof, and a gilt ball with a bronze banner and cardinal cross above that.
#
# WHERE IT STANDS. clips/estate/BRIEF.md puts the bell tower north-east of the facility on the
# cross axis, x ~= 30, z ~= 14. This clip is built in ITS OWN coordinates with its origin on the
# tower's centre line at the level of the lawn and of the tower's own ground floor, so it is
# stamped into the estate at (30, 0, 14) with no rotation. North is +z and east is +x, the estate's
# own sense, and the WEST face carries the door — which is the face that looks back at the
# facility — while the SOUTH face carries the clock.
#
# THE BOX, AND THE ARITHMETIC THE BRIEF ASKS FOR IN WRITING. bounds are 11 x 36 x 11 m, which at
# `metre 32` is 352 x 1152 x 352 = 142,737,408 cells, five bytes a cell in the game: 714 MB. That
# is affordable and it is why this building can be authored at full detail. Every bound is a whole
# number of voxels from the origin (11.0 and 36.0 are both exact multiples of 1/32 and the origin
# falls on a grid LINE in x, y and z), which is not decoration: the vane's banner is 0.03 m thick
# against a 0.03125 m voxel, so whether it exists at all depends on a cell centre landing inside
# it. It is written from 0.00 to 0.03 in z and not from -0.015 to +0.015 for exactly that reason.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — this is the half of what it is FOR, and it comes first.
# ================================================================================================
#
# 1. THE TALLEST THIN SILHOUETTE IN THE PROJECT, AND THE ONLY ONE SEEN END-ON. 5.40 m of shaft
#    carried 34.65 m into the air. From anywhere else on the estate this object is a vertical band
#    a few pixels wide with a complicated crown on it, and everything that makes it a building
#    rather than a post — the lesene strips (0.135 proud), the corbel-table arches (0.18 radius),
#    the quoins, the slits 0.30 wide — is sub-pixel until you walk up to it. There is no harder
#    test of continuous detail than a level-of-detail transition on a vertical edge against sky,
#    and this part is one long one. The belfry is the worst of it: eight openings, four colonnettes
#    0.30 across and twenty iron louvres 0.03 thick, all of which have to resolve out of one grey
#    smudge without a step.
#
# 2. A DEEP DARK SHAFT WITH ONE SLIT PER ALTERNATE STAGE. The stair well is 4.05 m square and
#    24.30 m tall — 400 cubic metres of interior reached through one door 1.35 wide and three slit
#    windows 0.30 wide, splayed from 0.30 outside to 0.90 inside through 0.675 of wall. The slits
#    are on stages one, three and five and on three different faces, so the light arrives on the
#    south at 2.25, on the east at 12.15 and on the north at 21.60 and there is nothing in between
#    but stone: no bounce surface in the whole shaft that is not sandstone or granite. This is the
#    crypt's problem stood on end, and the light has 24 m to travel down it.
#
# 3. THIN FLAT METAL AGAINST BRIGHT SKY, AT THE HIGHEST POINT IN THE SCENE. The banner is 0.03 m
#    thick — ONE VOXEL at metre 32 — 1.35 m across, at 34.65 m, with nothing behind it but sky, and
#    the cardinal cross beneath it is the same. It is NOT thickened and it must not be: this is the
#    hardest edge anywhere in the project and the only place to find out what the renderer does
#    with one. It is placed on the grid deliberately (see the box note above).
#
# 4. AN OPEN STAGE WITH A LARGE CURVED METAL BODY IN IT. The bell is bronze, 1.35 m across the
#    mouth, hollow, with a 0.09 wall, hanging on an oak headstock in a chamber open on four sides
#    at 26.55 m. It is lit from four directions at once and every one of its curved surfaces
#    reflects a different quarter of the estate. Two `lamp` lanterns stand with it, so at night the
#    top of this tower is a small bright thing 27 m up and everything below it is black.
#
# ================================================================================================
# THE SPIRAL, AND WHETHER IT GOT BOTH ITS RISE AND ITS RUN — the brief asks; here is the answer.
# ================================================================================================
#
# IT GOT BOTH, EXACTLY, AND THE SHAFT IS THE REASON. On a spiral the run is measured along the
# WALKING LINE, and the walking line of this stair is a circle. 0.18 of rise and 0.32 of run give
# a pitch of atan(0.18/0.32) = 29.36 degrees, the facility's own pitch; what a spiral adds is that
# the number of treads in a turn is then fixed by the radius, and it has to come out WHOLE or the
# stair does not close on itself.
#
#   internal shaft            4.05 m (9 M) square, so the wall face is 2.025 from the axis
#   walking line              0.35 m in from the wall face, which the brief names: r = 1.675
#   treads in a turn at 0.32  2 pi r / 0.32 = 32.87
#
# 32.87 is not a whole number, so ONE of the three has to move. The rise may not (0.18 everywhere,
# without exception) and the run may not (0.32, the same). So the WALKING LINE moves: 33 treads to
# the turn puts the line at r = 33 x 0.32 / 2 pi = 1.68068, which is 0.344 m in from the wall face
# instead of 0.350. Six millimetres. The rise is 0.18 and the run is 0.32 and neither was fudged.
#
# WHAT IS NOT 0.32, and saying so is the point of the paragraph. A tread on a spiral is a wedge, so
# it has ONE going of 0.32 and every other radius is a different number:
#
#   at the newel face   r = 0.675   going 0.1285      at the wall face  r = 2.025   going 0.3856
#
# That is what a spiral stair IS — it is not a defect and there is no radius at which it is not
# true. What it does decide is the newel: the newel is 1.35 across (3 M, r = 0.675) and not the
# 0.45 post it could have been, because at r = 0.225 the inner going would be 0.043 — one voxel at
# metre 32 — and the inner end of every tread would have vanished into speckle. At 0.675 the
# narrow end of a tread is 0.1285, which is four voxels, and it is buried in the newel besides.
#
# 135 RISERS FROM 0.00 TO 24.30, so 134 treads and the belfry floor is the 135th riser. A turn is
# 33 x 0.18 = 5.94 m, so the whole stair is 4.061 turns, and the treads are placed by DOUBLING a
# rotation and a lift together — the technique clips/facility/stair.clip uses on its balustrade,
# for the same reason: every tread is then the same shape carried by an isometry, and an isometry
# cannot drift. Thirty-three of them make one turn; the turn is doubled twice for 132 and the last
# two are written out. Nine lines for a hundred and thirty-four treads.
#
# ================================================================================================
# HEAD HEIGHT, AND WHY `walkability` CANNOT SEE ANY OF IT
# ================================================================================================
#
# clips/facility/stair.clip's header says it and it is worth saying again here because a spiral is
# the case it was written about: measure.cpp's `walkability` walks a HEIGHTFIELD — for every column
# of voxels it finds the topmost matter and asks for clear air above it. A floor under an overhang
# is never the topmost matter in its column, so it is never tested. EVERY TREAD OF A SPIRAL IS
# UNDER AN OVERHANG. The engine has nothing to say about this stair. So this file measures it
# itself, and the measurement is a binding anybody can run:
#
#     ./build/clipcheck clips/estate/campanile.clip --metre 32 --part campanile_probe_head
#
# It MUST report an empty clip. It is the volume a person's head sweeps through every place this
# tower lets them stand — 2.10 m over every one of the 134 treads, over the ground floor and over
# the belfry floor, and 2.40 m through the door — INTERSECTED with the tower itself.
#
# THE THREE NUMBERS THE PROBE DECIDED, none of which is obvious and all of which move if somebody
# tidies this file:
#
#   THE SPANDREL UNDER THE FIRST THIRTEEN TREADS. A turn of this stair rises 5.94 m, so the tread
#   directly overhead is never the problem — the clearance under it is 5.94 - 0.27 = 5.67 m, the
#   most generous head height in the project. The problem is the BOTTOM of the flight, where the
#   first thirteen treads come down to the floor and the wedge under them is between zero and
#   2.25 m high. There is no honest way to let anybody in there, so it is FILLED, one solid sector
#   per tread, exactly as stair.clip fills the wedge under its lower flight. That takes the whole
#   volume out of the probe by making it not a place, and it leaves the ground floor 218 degrees of
#   a 4.05 m square with a worst head height of 2.25 m — tread fourteen's soffit, 2.52 - 0.27.
#   FIFTEEN CENTIMETRES OF MARGIN, and it is the reason the treads are 0.27 thick (0.6 M) and not
#   the 0.36 that would have looked better in section: 0.36 gives 2.16 and one weathering coat.
#
#   THE DOOR IS ON THE WEST. It could not be on the east or the south: the filled spandrel occupies
#   the sector from ENE clockwise through E, SE and S to SW, so those faces open into solid stone.
#   West is where the open floor is, and it is also the face that looks at the facility, so this is
#   a constraint that happened to agree with the elevation. The head over the doorway inside is the
#   soffit of treads eighteen to twenty-one, 2.97 m and up.
#
#   THE BELFRY FLOOR IS THE SOUTH HALF ONLY, AND THE STAIR IS SET SO ITS LAST TREAD ENDS ON THE
#   z = 0 LINE. 2.10 m of head under a floor at 24.30 needs 2.10 / 5.94 = 0.354 of a TURN of that
#   floor to be open — thirteen treads, 141.8 degrees — or the last thirteen treads of the climb
#   are under a ceiling that is 0.27 above them at the top. So the well is not a hatch, it is half
#   the chamber. 33 = 3 x 11, so a tread edge lands exactly on a cardinal direction every eleven
#   treads: the whole helix is turned by -2/33 of a turn so that tread 134's front edge lies on the
#   +x axis, the well is the north half (z >= 0) and the floor is the south half, both of them
#   module-clean, and you step off the last tread southward onto the floor with 0.18 to climb. The
#   thirteen treads that need the opening span 218.2 to 360 degrees, every one of which has z >= 0.
#   Turn the helix by anything else and the floor has to be a sector, cut by hand, and wrong.
#
# ================================================================================================
# THE MODULE — every dimension below is one of these or a simple fraction of one
# ================================================================================================
#
#   D = 0.90   M = 0.45   riser 0.18 (0.4 M)   run 0.32
#
#   PLAN                                        LEVELS
#   shaft outer     +-2.70    12 M square       ground / floor         0.00
#   shaft inner     +-2.025    9 M square       plinth step 1          0.18
#   wall             0.675   1.5 M              plinth step 2          0.36
#   newel            r 0.675   3 M across       stage bands  4.95 9.90 14.85 19.80  (11 M apart)
#   plinth step 2   +-3.15                      belfry floor          24.30   54 M
#   plinth step 1   +-3.60                      belfry sill           25.20   +2 M
#   cornice         +-3.15                      arch springing        27.675  +5.5 M
#   roof base       +-3.15                      arch crown            28.35   +7 M
#   belfry pier      1.125   2.5 M              belfry top            28.80   +10 M
#   belfry opening   1.35      3 M              cornice top           29.25   +M
#   colonnette       0.30                       roof apex             33.30   +9 M
#   vane banner      1.35 x 0.45 x 0.03         vane top              34.65   +3 M

include "../facility/_contract.clip"

metre 32
bounds -5.5 -1.0 -5.5   5.5 35.0 5.5

# A KEY MUST NOT START A LINE, and it is worth knowing before you wrap anything. The parser is
# token-based everywhere except here: `keys_into` stops at any token that STARTS a line, because
# that is how it knows one statement has ended and the next begun. So `offset { a }` on one line
# and `by=-0.05` on the next is not `offset by -0.05`, it is an offset with no `by` followed by
# three tokens of nonsense — and the errors it produces name a line thirty statements earlier. A
# key goes on the same line as the brace it belongs to, or the shape gets its own `let`.
#
# ================================================================================================
# THE HALF SPACES — every plane in this file is one of these four, turned and carried
# ================================================================================================
#
# There is no way in this language to say "the wedge between two radial planes", and a spiral stair
# is nothing but that, a hundred and thirty-four times. A half space IS sayable — a box far larger
# than the clip, with one face on the origin — and `rotate` is an isometry, so a rotated half space
# is exact and keeps an honest bounding box where a hand-written `plane` would keep neither.
#
# THE SENSE, WHICH IS THE THING TO GET RIGHT ONCE AND THEN NEVER THINK ABOUT AGAIN. `rotate` turns
# the SHAPE by the positive angle, and about y it carries +x toward -z. So with the angle phi
# measured in TURNS from +x in that direction, a point at phi lies at (cos, -sin) in (x, z), and
#
#     intersection { hs_zneg  rotate { hs_zpos } y=b }        is the sector phi in [0, b]
#     intersection { rotate { hs_zneg } y=a  rotate { hs_zpos } y=b }   is phi in [a, b]
#
# because hs_zneg is phi in [0, 0.5] and hs_zpos rotated by b is phi in [b - 0.5, b]. Checked
# against a slice before a line of the tower was written, not reasoned about and hoped for.
let camp_hs_xneg = box -60 -60 -60     0  60  60
let camp_hs_xpos = box   0 -60 -60    60  60  60
let camp_hs_yneg = box -60 -60 -60    60   0  60
let camp_hs_ypos = box -60   0 -60    60  60  60
let camp_hs_zneg = box -60 -60 -60    60  60   0
let camp_hs_zpos = box -60 -60   0    60  60  60

# ================================================================================================
# THE GROUND — lawn, with a gravel apron round the tower's foot
# ================================================================================================
#
# A clip is its own world, so this one has to stand on something. The lawn's top face is y = 0.00,
# which is the estate's datum and also this tower's own ground floor, so a person walks in over a
# threshold and not up a step.
let camp_lawn   = box -5.45 -0.60 -5.45   5.45 0.00 5.45
let camp_gravel = box -4.95 -0.30 -4.95   4.95 0.00 4.95
let camp_ground = union { camp_lawn camp_gravel }

# ================================================================================================
# THE PLINTH AND THE SHAFT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Two granite steps, 0.18 each — the building's own riser, so nothing here is a rise the
# walkability check has to think about. Their treads are 0.45 (M) and not the stair's 0.32: this is
# a stylobate and not a flight, and a tread DEEPER than a run is the generous direction. The
# doorway is cut straight through both of them on the west, which is what a plinth does.
#
# Written 0.01 inside every face with `round=0.01`, the idiom stair.clip explains at length: round
# GROWS a box, so a box written to 24.29 has its top at 24.30. Every box in this file that carries
# a round is written short by the round.
let camp_plinth_1 = box -3.59 -0.60 -3.59   3.59 0.17 3.59  round=0.01
let camp_plinth_2 = box -3.14 -0.60 -3.14   3.14 0.35 3.14  round=0.01
let camp_shaft    = box -2.69 -0.60 -2.69   2.69 24.29 2.69 round=0.01

# THE QUOINS. A dressed limestone corner post 0.45 wide (M) on each face of each corner, standing
# 0.045 proud of the ashlar — one quoin block written at the +x +z corner and folded to all four,
# because `mirror` takes the absolute value of a coordinate and a shape drawn on +x therefore
# appears on both sides of the axis. Two mirrors, four corners, one shape.
let camp_quoin_one = box 2.245 -0.60 2.245   2.735 24.29 2.735 round=0.01
let camp_quoins    = mirror { mirror { camp_quoin_one } axis=z } axis=x

# THE LESENE STRIPS — the Lombard manner. Thin flat pilaster strips 0.45 wide and 0.135 proud,
# running the WHOLE height of the shaft from the plinth to the belfry, two to a face, at +-1.125
# from the centre of each face. They divide every face into three panels of 0.90, 1.80 and 0.90,
# and those three numbers are why the corbel arcade below has a pitch of 0.45 and comes out whole
# in all three: two arches, four arches, two arches, eight to a face.
let camp_lesene_x = box 2.60 0.30 0.90   2.825 24.29 1.34  round=0.01
let camp_lesene_z = box 0.90 0.30 2.60   1.34 24.29 2.825  round=0.01
let camp_lesene_one = union { camp_lesene_x camp_lesene_z }
let camp_lesenes = mirror { mirror { camp_lesene_one } axis=z } axis=x

# THE LOMBARD BANDS — a corbel table at the top of every stage, which is what joins the strips.
#
# A band 0.45 tall standing 0.09 proud, with a row of small semicircular arches cut into its
# underside at 0.45 centres, radius 0.18. The cut reaches 0.105 INTO the wall face, so the arches
# read as a blind arcade recessed behind the band rather than as scalloping on a fillet. Four of
# them, at 4.95, 9.90, 14.85 and 19.80 — 11 M apart — and a fifth at 24.30 under the belfry.
#
# The arch centres are at 0.225, 0.675, 1.575 and 2.025 from the middle of a face and NOT at 1.125,
# because that is where a lesene stands: the arcade runs BETWEEN the strips, in the three panels,
# which is the whole point of the motif. Four cuts written on +x, folded to +-z and then to +-x.
let camp_arch_cut_a = cylinder 2.745 0 0.225 r=0.18 h=0.30 axis=x
let camp_arch_cut_b = cylinder 2.745 0 0.675 r=0.18 h=0.30 axis=x
let camp_arch_cut_c = cylinder 2.745 0 1.575 r=0.18 h=0.30 axis=x
let camp_arch_cut_d = cylinder 2.745 0 2.025 r=0.18 h=0.30 axis=x
let camp_arch_row_x = mirror { union { camp_arch_cut_a camp_arch_cut_b
                                       camp_arch_cut_c camp_arch_cut_d } } axis=z
let camp_arch_cut_e = cylinder 0.225 0 2.745 r=0.18 h=0.30 axis=z
let camp_arch_cut_f = cylinder 0.675 0 2.745 r=0.18 h=0.30 axis=z
let camp_arch_cut_g = cylinder 1.575 0 2.745 r=0.18 h=0.30 axis=z
let camp_arch_cut_h = cylinder 2.025 0 2.745 r=0.18 h=0.30 axis=z
let camp_arch_row_z = mirror { union { camp_arch_cut_e camp_arch_cut_f
                                       camp_arch_cut_g camp_arch_cut_h } } axis=x
let camp_arch_row = mirror { mirror { union { camp_arch_row_x camp_arch_row_z } } axis=z } axis=x

let camp_band_1 = box -2.78 4.50 -2.78   2.78 4.94 2.78  round=0.01
let camp_band_2 = box -2.78 9.45 -2.78   2.78 9.89 2.78  round=0.01
let camp_band_3 = box -2.78 14.40 -2.78  2.78 14.84 2.78 round=0.01
let camp_band_4 = box -2.78 19.35 -2.78  2.78 19.79 2.78 round=0.01
let camp_band_5 = box -2.78 23.85 -2.78  2.78 24.29 2.78 round=0.01
let camp_bands_solid = union { camp_band_1 camp_band_2 camp_band_3 camp_band_4 camp_band_5 }

let camp_arches_1 = translate { camp_arch_row } 0 4.50 0
let camp_arches_2 = translate { camp_arch_row } 0 9.45 0
let camp_arches_3 = translate { camp_arch_row } 0 14.40 0
let camp_arches_4 = translate { camp_arch_row } 0 19.35 0
let camp_arches_5 = translate { camp_arch_row } 0 23.85 0
let camp_arch_cuts = union { camp_arches_1 camp_arches_2 camp_arches_3
                             camp_arches_4 camp_arches_5 }

let camp_bands = difference { camp_bands_solid camp_arch_cuts }

# ================================================================================================
# THE BELFRY, THE CORNICE AND THE ROOF — the masses; the openings are cut further down
# ================================================================================================
let camp_belfry  = box -2.69 24.10 -2.69   2.69 28.79 2.69 round=0.01
let camp_cornice = box -3.14 28.60 -3.14   3.14 29.24 3.14 round=0.01

# The pyramid, as four sloping half spaces met over a base. Base half width 3.15, apex 33.30, so
# the rise over the run is 4.05 : 3.15 and the plane's own turn is atan(3.15/4.05) / tau =
# 0.10520829. Each half space is turned by that and then CARRIED TO A POINT ON THE PLANE — the
# middle of its own eaves — which is the placement stair.clip's soffit note argues for: an offset
# perpendicular to a rake is not the same number as an offset below it, and the difference here
# would be 120 mm of eaves.
let camp_roof_e = translate { rotate { camp_hs_xneg } z=0.10520829 }   3.15 29.25 0
let camp_roof_w = translate { rotate { camp_hs_xpos } z=-0.10520829 } -3.15 29.25 0
let camp_roof_n = translate { rotate { camp_hs_zneg } x=-0.10520829 }  0 29.25 3.15
let camp_roof_s = translate { rotate { camp_hs_zpos } x=0.10520829 }   0 29.25 -3.15
let camp_roof_solid = intersection { box -3.70 29.10 -3.70  3.70 33.31 3.70
                                     camp_roof_e camp_roof_w camp_roof_n camp_roof_s }

# The copper is a SKIN and not a solid. 0.09 thick (0.2 M, three voxels at metre 32), open at the
# eaves so the bell chamber sees up into it — which is what makes the belfry a 90 cubic metre
# hollow lit only through its own arches rather than a room with a lid on it.
let camp_roof_skin = difference { shell { camp_roof_solid } thickness=0.045
                                  box -6 -2 -6   6 29.15 6 }

# The oak the copper is laid on: four hip rafters up the arrises, and a king post at the apex the
# vane's spindle is bedded in.
let camp_hip_one = capsule 3.00 29.05 3.00   0 33.10 0  r=0.09
let camp_hips    = mirror { mirror { camp_hip_one } axis=z } axis=x
let camp_kingpost = cylinder 0 32.60 0 r=0.135 h=1.40 axis=y
let camp_roof_oak = union { camp_hips camp_kingpost }

# ================================================================================================
# WHAT IS CUT OUT — the well, the door, the slits, the belfry openings, the cornice's own eye
# ================================================================================================

# The well: 4.05 m square, the full height of the shaft, and on up through the belfry.
#
# THE TWO OVERLAP BY 0.40 AND THEY MUST, and it is the one seam in this file that no picture would
# have found. Written to meet EXACTLY at 24.30 they carve the same air — the geometry is identical
# either way — but the VOID'S OWN SDF is then nought on that whole plane, and a difference reads
# `max(solid, -void)`, so the carved field just touches zero across the entire 4.05 m square
# instead of staying clear of it. Add 16 mm of grain to a field that touches zero and half of it
# goes negative: a sheet of speckled stone ONE VOXEL THICK hanging in the stair well at 24.30, 144
# voxels in the largest piece, 796 in all, and every one of them a floating component. Overlapping
# the two boxes puts the void's distance at -0.20 there instead of nought and the sheet is gone.
let camp_well   = box -2.025 0.00 -2.025   2.025 24.50 2.025
let camp_chamber = box -2.025 24.10 -2.025  2.025 28.85 2.025

# The cornice's eye, so the bell chamber opens into the roof rather than stopping at a lid.
let camp_cornice_eye = box -2.025 28.70 -2.025   2.025 29.30 2.025

# THE DOOR — west face, 1.35 wide (3 M) and 2.70 tall (6 M) to the crown of a round head, which is
# 0.30 over the 2.40 the brief asks for through a door. Cut clear out through both plinth steps.
let camp_door_jamb = box -3.80 0.00 -0.675   -1.90 2.025 0.675
let camp_door_head = cylinder -2.85 2.025 0 r=0.675 h=1.95 axis=x
let camp_door = union { camp_door_jamb camp_door_head }

# THE SLIT WINDOWS — 0.30 wide outside, 0.90 inside, splayed through 0.675 of wall, and taller
# inside than out by 0.45 at each end. Four sloping planes and a slab, written ONCE at the origin
# with the outer face on z = 0 and the wall running to +z, and then carried and turned to the three
# faces that have one. The splay angles are atan(0.30/0.675) across and atan(0.45/0.675) up.
let camp_slit_l = translate { rotate { camp_hs_xpos } y=-0.06656247 } -0.15 0 0
let camp_slit_r = translate { rotate { camp_hs_xneg } y=0.06656247 }   0.15 0 0
let camp_slit_b = translate { rotate { camp_hs_ypos } x=0.09358352 }   0 0 0
let camp_slit_t = translate { rotate { camp_hs_yneg } x=-0.09358352 }  0 1.35 0
let camp_slit = intersection { box -1.2 -1.0 -0.20   1.2 2.6 0.90
                               camp_slit_l camp_slit_r camp_slit_b camp_slit_t }

# Stage one on the south, stage three on the east, stage five on the north. Sills at 2.25, 12.15
# and 21.60 — 5 M, 27 M and 48 M. The clock takes the south face of stage five, so the fifth-stage
# slit is put opposite it; and the three faces are three different ones on purpose, because the
# point of the shaft is that its light arrives a stage at a time and from a different side each
# time.
# The base slit is drawn with its narrow outer aperture on z = 0 and the wall running toward +z,
# so it belongs UNTURNED on the south face, where the outside is at -2.70 and the inside at -2.025.
# The north face is the same thing turned a half turn, the east face three quarters.
let camp_slit_s = translate { camp_slit }                    0 2.25 -2.70
let camp_slit_e = translate { rotate { camp_slit } y=0.75 }  2.70 12.15 0
let camp_slit_n = translate { rotate { camp_slit } y=0.5 }   0 21.60 2.70
let camp_slits = union { camp_slit_s camp_slit_e camp_slit_n }

# THE BELFRY OPENINGS — the biforate window, which is the motif this tower exists for.
#
# Two openings 1.35 wide (3 M) and 3.15 tall (7 M) to the crown, divided by a colonnette, in each
# of four faces. Corner piers 1.125 (2.5 M), colonnette bay 0.45 (M): 1.125 + 1.35 + 0.45 + 1.35 +
# 1.125 = 5.40, which is the face. Sill at 25.20, which is 0.90 (2 M) over the belfry floor — an
# upstand, not a trip: measure.cpp reads any rise between 0.19 and 0.75 as a step somebody meant to
# take, and 0.90 is a wall.
#
# Written for the +z face and turned by `around count=4`, which is sound here because the whole
# void lies inside 40 degrees of that face's own 90 degree sector.
let camp_belf_light = box -1.575 25.20 1.90   1.575 27.675 2.80
let camp_belf_head_a = cylinder -0.90 27.675 2.35 r=0.675 h=0.90 axis=z
let camp_belf_head_b = cylinder  0.90 27.675 2.35 r=0.675 h=0.90 axis=z
let camp_belf_bay = union { camp_belf_light camp_belf_head_a camp_belf_head_b }
let camp_belf_voids = around { camp_belf_bay } count=4 axis=y

# THE CLOCK'S RECESS is cut with the rest of the voids and so has to be bound here, above them;
# everything else about the clock is built with the fittings further down.
let camp_clock_recess = cylinder 0 22.05 -2.59 r=1.395 h=0.32 axis=z

let camp_voids = union { camp_well camp_chamber camp_cornice_eye camp_door
                         camp_slits camp_belf_voids camp_clock_recess }

# ================================================================================================
# THE CARVE — and everything that stands INSIDE the shaft goes back after it
# ================================================================================================
#
# The long note at the bottom of clips/facility.clip is about exactly this: a fitting stands in
# somebody's air by definition, so subtracting the air of a room deletes the furniture in it. The
# stair, the newel, the belfry floor, the bell and the lanterns all stand inside `camp_well` and
# `camp_chamber`, and if they are unioned before the difference the shaft eats every one of them.
# They are built below and unioned AFTER.
let camp_masonry = union { camp_shaft camp_plinth_1 camp_plinth_2 camp_quoins
                           camp_lesenes camp_bands camp_belfry camp_cornice }
let camp_carved = difference { camp_masonry camp_voids }

# ================================================================================================
# THE NEWEL AND THE GROUND FLOOR
# ================================================================================================
#
# The newel is 1.35 across (3 M) and runs the whole height of the shaft, its top flush with the
# belfry floor. FLUSH, and not a post standing proud of it: a finial 0.18 over a floor is a 0.18
# rise beside it, which is this building's own riser and would make the newel the worst step in the
# tower. See stair.clip's note on its own newels for the same arithmetic.
let camp_newel = cylinder 0 11.85 0 r=0.675 h=24.90 axis=y
let camp_floor = box -2.075 -0.60 -2.075   2.075 0.00 2.075

# ================================================================================================
# THE SPIRAL STAIR — 134 treads, 0.18 and 0.32, placed by doubling a rotation and a lift together
# ================================================================================================
#
# ONE TREAD, and everything else is that tread carried by an isometry. It is a wedge: a disc 5.80
# across caught between two radial planes 1.3/33 of a turn apart, squared off to the well so it
# runs into all four walls and into the corners, and 0.27 thick (0.6 M).
#
# 1.3/33 AND NOT 1/33, and the extra 0.3 is not sloppiness — it is what makes the flight one solid.
# The sector runs 0.3 of a tread FORWARD, in the direction of the climb, where it is buried under
# the next tread up and cannot be seen or stepped on; the tread's own exposed going stays exactly
# 1/33 of a turn, because the tread above starts at 1/33 and covers everything past it. Extending
# BACKWARD instead would have overhung the tread below and eaten 0.096 m off the going of every
# tread in the tower, which is the one mistake here that no picture would show.
#
# 0.27 THICK IS ALSO ARITHMETIC. Consecutive treads are 0.18 apart, so a slab thinner than 0.18
# leaves a slot between a tread and its neighbour's forward extension — a hole through the stair,
# the whole way up. 0.27 overlaps by 0.09 (0.2 M) and there is nothing to fall through. It is also
# what sets the ground floor's head height: see the spandrel note in the header.
#
# The tread runs from r = 0 rather than from the newel face, and the wedge it makes near the axis
# is thin and ugly and is INSIDE the newel, which is solid there. A tread that stopped at the newel
# face would have had to be bedded into it by hand; this one is bedded by construction.
let camp_tread_disc = cylinder 0 0.045 0 r=2.89 h=0.25 axis=y
let camp_tread_sq   = box -2.065 -0.30 -2.065   2.065 0.30 2.065
let camp_tread_sec  = intersection { camp_hs_zneg  rotate { camp_hs_zpos } y=0.03939394 }
let camp_tread = round { intersection { camp_tread_disc camp_tread_sq camp_tread_sec } } by=0.01

# The doubling. Each line is the line above it, plus the line above it turned one whole block of
# treads round the newel and lifted the same block of risers. 1/33 of a turn is 0.03030303.
let camp_tr_01 = camp_tread
let camp_tr_02 = union { camp_tr_01 translate { rotate { camp_tr_01 } y=0.03030303 } 0 0.18 0 }
let camp_tr_04 = union { camp_tr_02 translate { rotate { camp_tr_02 } y=0.06060606 } 0 0.36 0 }
let camp_tr_08 = union { camp_tr_04 translate { rotate { camp_tr_04 } y=0.12121212 } 0 0.72 0 }
let camp_tr_16 = union { camp_tr_08 translate { rotate { camp_tr_08 } y=0.24242424 } 0 1.44 0 }
let camp_tr_32 = union { camp_tr_16 translate { rotate { camp_tr_16 } y=0.48484848 } 0 2.88 0 }
let camp_tr_33 = union { camp_tr_32 translate { rotate { camp_tr_01 } y=0.96969697 } 0 5.76 0 }

# A turn is 33 treads and 5.94 m, and 33/33 is a whole turn, so the turns are pure LIFTS with no
# rotation at all — which is the one place in this file where a number being whole is worth
# something beyond tidiness.
let camp_tr_66  = union { camp_tr_33 translate { camp_tr_33 } 0 5.94 0 }
let camp_tr_132 = union { camp_tr_66 translate { camp_tr_66 } 0 11.88 0 }
let camp_tr_134 = union { camp_tr_132
                          translate { camp_tr_01 } 0 23.76 0
                          translate { rotate { camp_tr_01 } y=0.03030303 } 0 23.94 0 }

# THE SPANDREL. The first thirteen treads, each carried down to the floor, so the wedge under the
# bottom of the flight is solid stone and not a place. One sector column per tread, capped at that
# tread's own top, which is what makes the fill meet the treads with no seam.
let camp_sp_col = intersection { cylinder 0 12 0 r=2.90 h=26 axis=y
                                 box -2.075 -0.60 -2.075   2.075 25.0 2.075
                                 camp_hs_zneg  rotate { camp_hs_zpos } y=0.03939394 }
let camp_sp_01 = intersection { camp_sp_col  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 0.18 3 }
let camp_sp_02 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.03030303  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 0.36 3 }
let camp_sp_03 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.06060606  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 0.54 3 }
let camp_sp_04 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.09090909  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 0.72 3 }
let camp_sp_05 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.12121212  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 0.90 3 }
let camp_sp_06 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.15151515  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 1.08 3 }
let camp_sp_07 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.18181818  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 1.26 3 }
let camp_sp_08 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.21212121  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 1.44 3 }
let camp_sp_09 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.24242424  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 1.62 3 }
let camp_sp_10 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.27272727  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 1.80 3 }
let camp_sp_11 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.30303030  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 1.98 3 }
let camp_sp_12 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.33333333  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 2.16 3 }
let camp_sp_13 = intersection { rotate { camp_sp_col } y=0.36363636  box -3 -0.60 -3  3 2.34 3 }
let camp_spandrel_raw = union {
    camp_sp_01 camp_sp_02 camp_sp_03 camp_sp_04 camp_sp_05 camp_sp_06 camp_sp_07
    camp_sp_08 camp_sp_09 camp_sp_10 camp_sp_11 camp_sp_12 camp_sp_13
}

# AND THE WHOLE HELIX IS TURNED BY -2/33 OF A TURN. That is the number worked out in the header:
# it puts tread 134's front edge on the +x axis, so the belfry's well is the north half of the
# chamber and its floor is the south half, and neither of them has to be a hand-cut sector.
let camp_stair    = rotate { camp_tr_134 }       y=-0.06060606
let camp_spandrel = rotate { camp_spandrel_raw } y=-0.06060606

# ================================================================================================
# THE BELFRY FLOOR AND ITS PARAPET
# ================================================================================================
#
# The south half of the chamber, 0.27 thick (the treads' own waist, so the two soffits are one
# plane where they meet), bedded 0.05 into all three walls it reaches. The north half is the well,
# and the header says at length why it has to be that big.
let camp_belf_floor = box -2.075 24.03 -2.075   2.075 24.30 0.00

# The well's parapet: 0.90 tall (2 M), stopping at x = 0.45 so the head of the stair is open — you
# arrive out of the north-east and step south onto the floor. 0.90 and not 0.45, for the reason the
# belfry sill is 0.90: anything between 0.19 and 0.75 is read as a step and this is a 24 m drop.
let camp_belf_parapet = box -2.075 24.20 -0.225   0.45 25.20 0.00

# ================================================================================================
# THE COLONNETTES — marble, one to a face, carrying the two arches over it
# ================================================================================================
#
# 0.30 across and 1.80 (4 M) in the shaft, on a square plinth and under a cushion capital: the
# Romanesque colonnette, and the only slender thing in the belfry that is stone rather than metal.
# The cushion is a sphere clipped square — narrow where it meets the round shaft, full 0.45 square
# where it meets the abacus, which is what a cushion capital is.
let camp_col_base  = box -0.1875 25.15 2.175   0.1875 25.425 2.55
let camp_col_shaft = cylinder 0 26.325 2.3625 r=0.15 h=1.90 axis=y
let camp_col_cush  = intersection { box -0.225 27.20 2.1375   0.225 27.585 2.5875
                                    sphere 0 27.585 2.3625 r=0.42 }
let camp_col_abacus = box -0.2475 27.575 2.115   0.2475 27.72 2.61
let camp_col_one = union { camp_col_base camp_col_shaft camp_col_cush camp_col_abacus }
let camp_colonnettes = around { camp_col_one } count=4 axis=y

# THE LOUVRES — five iron slats to a head, 0.03 thick, tilted a sixteenth of a turn.
#
# Thin matter at the top of a tower, seen against sky through an opening: 0.03 is one voxel at
# metre 32 and is meant to be. The stack is built at the ORIGIN and carried, because `repeat` folds
# a point toward the origin and a shape written where it stands is never found by it — the mistake
# stair.clip avoids by repeating only along an axis its child sits on.
let camp_louvre_slat = box -1.60 -0.015 -0.15   1.60 0.015 0.15
let camp_louvre_stack = translate {
    repeat { rotate { camp_louvre_slat } x=0.05 } y=0.135 ny=2
} 0 28.0125 2.38
# CLIPPED TO THE HEADS GROWN BY 0.05 AND NOT TO THE HEADS THEMSELVES. The arch head IS the void,
# so a slat clipped to it ends exactly where the masonry begins and the two touch on a surface with
# no volume in common — which is the hairline the brief warns about, and twenty louvres reported as
# twenty floating components. `offset` with a NEGATIVE value grows (stair.clip's note on the sign),
# so this bites each slat 0.05 into the voussoirs at both ends.
let camp_louvre_heads = offset { union { camp_belf_head_a camp_belf_head_b } } by=-0.05
let camp_louvre_one = intersection { camp_louvre_stack camp_louvre_heads }
let camp_louvres = around { camp_louvre_one } count=4 axis=y

# ================================================================================================
# THE BELL — bronze, 1.35 across the mouth, hollow, on an oak headstock
# ================================================================================================
#
# Drawn as a MERIDIAN and revolved, which is the only way to get a bell: `revolve` folds a point to
# (radius, height) and asks the profile there, so the answer is a true distance and the surface is
# exact rather than a stack of drums. The flare is the outside of a circle of radius 1.548 centred
# well clear of the axis — a bell's waist is concave seen from outside, and a cone is not a bell.
#
# The mouth is at 26.55, which is 2.25 over the belfry floor: the bell hangs on the tower's axis
# and half of it is therefore over the floor rather than over the well, so its mouth is a head
# height like any other and the probe tests it.
let camp_bell_flare = intersection { box 0.00 0.00 -1  0.72 0.90 1
                                     difference { box -2 -2 -1  4 4 1
                                                  sphere 1.897 0.95 0 r=1.5479 } }
let camp_bell_shoulder = intersection { sphere 0 0.90 0 r=0.375  box 0 0.86 -1  1 1.30 1 }
let camp_bell_crown = box 0.00 1.05 -1   0.18 1.22 1
let camp_bell_profile = union { camp_bell_flare camp_bell_shoulder camp_bell_crown }

# THE BORE IS A SECOND PROFILE AND NOT AN `offset` OF THE SOLID, and that is the difference between
# a bell and a lump. `offset { bell } by=0.09` shrinks the whole body 0.09 in EVERY direction —
# including UP FROM THE MOUTH — so the cavity starts 0.09 above the lip and the difference leaves a
# 0.09 bronze plate right across the mouth. A closed bell, and from outside it looks perfect.
#
# The bore is therefore the same two curves drawn again 0.09 nearer the axis: the flare's circle
# with its centre moved 0.09 IN (1.807 rather than 1.897, which moves the arc in by exactly 0.09 at
# every height), the shoulder's sphere 0.09 smaller, and the box carried below the mouth to y=-0.40
# so the mouth is open. It stops at 1.185, which leaves the crown solid — a bell is solid where its
# canons are, and it is what the clapper staple hangs from.
let camp_bell_bore_flare = intersection { box 0.00 -0.40 -1  0.63 0.86 1
                                          difference { box -2 -2 -1  4 4 1
                                                       sphere 1.807 0.95 0 r=1.5479 } }
let camp_bell_bore_shoulder = intersection { sphere 0 0.90 0 r=0.285  box 0 0.80 -1  1 1.30 1 }
let camp_bell_bore = union { camp_bell_bore_flare camp_bell_bore_shoulder }
let camp_bell_section = difference { camp_bell_profile camp_bell_bore }
let camp_bell = translate { revolve { camp_bell_section } axis=y } 0 26.55 0

# The clapper: an iron staple in the SOLID crown — 27.80 and not 27.62, because the bore reaches
# 27.735 and a staple that stopped inside the cavity would hang the clapper off nothing — a shank,
# and a ball at the sound bow.
let camp_clapper_shank = capsule 0 27.80 0   0 26.90 0  r=0.045
let camp_clapper_ball  = sphere 0 26.80 0 r=0.135
let camp_clapper = union { camp_clapper_shank camp_clapper_ball }

# The headstock and the beam it swings from. The beam is bedded 0.05 into the east and west walls
# at z = 0, where the masonry between the two arch heads is solid — checked against the arch
# circles rather than assumed: the heads are radius 0.675 about z = +-0.90, so z = 0 is 0.225 clear
# of both.
let camp_headstock = box -0.55 27.70 -0.15   0.55 28.00 0.15  round=0.01
let camp_bell_beam = box -2.075 27.95 -0.135   2.075 28.31 0.135 round=0.01
let camp_bell_strap_l = box -0.42 27.55 -0.19   -0.36 28.05 0.19
let camp_bell_strap_r = box  0.36 27.55 -0.19    0.42 28.05 0.19
let camp_bell_straps = union { camp_bell_strap_l camp_bell_strap_r }

# ================================================================================================
# THE LANTERNS — two, so the top of the tower is a small bright thing in the sky at night
# ================================================================================================
#
# On the two south inner corners of the bell chamber, bracketed off the corner, with the whole
# lantern from 26.55 up — clear of 26.40, which is 2.10 over the belfry floor, so a lantern is a
# light and not a thing to walk into. The probe tests that too.
let camp_lant_bracket = capsule 1.99 27.22 -1.99   1.80 27.10 -1.80  r=0.045
let camp_lant_cap  = prism 1.80 27.15 -1.80 r=0.21 h=0.14 sides=8 axis=y
let camp_lant_foot = prism 1.80 26.62 -1.80 r=0.21 h=0.14 sides=8 axis=y
let camp_lant_glass = prism 1.80 26.90 -1.80 r=0.15 h=0.48 sides=8 axis=y
let camp_lant_frame = union { camp_lant_bracket camp_lant_cap camp_lant_foot }
let camp_lanterns_frame = mirror { camp_lant_frame } axis=x
let camp_lanterns_glass = mirror { camp_lant_glass } axis=x
let camp_lanterns = union { camp_lanterns_frame camp_lanterns_glass }

# ================================================================================================
# THE CLOCK — south face, top stage of the shaft, 2.70 across
# ================================================================================================
#
# AND IT IS ON THE SHAFT AND NOT ON THE BELFRY, which is a departure from the brief and is said
# here rather than hidden. The belfry stage has no 2.70 m of blind wall on any face and cannot
# have one: 1.125 + 1.35 + 0.45 + 1.35 + 1.125 is the whole of a 5.40 face, and the only way to
# find 2.70 of masonry up there is to take out one of the biforate openings — which is the motif
# the tower exists for. So the clock goes on the face immediately below, centred at 22.05 on the
# fifth stage, where there is blind wall by the metre and where every tower that has both a clock
# and a bell chamber actually puts it.
# The recess is 0.27 deep into a 0.675 wall and is cut with the rest of the voids; the marble
# disc is bedded 0.05 past the back of it, so the face reads as sunk behind a bronze bezel that
# stands 0.09 proud of the ashlar rather than as a disc stuck on a wall.
let camp_clock_disc   = cylinder 0 22.05 -2.495 r=1.35 h=0.23 axis=z
let camp_clock_bezel  = difference { cylinder 0 22.05 -2.595 r=1.395 h=0.39 axis=z
                                     cylinder 0 22.05 -2.595 r=1.26 h=0.50 axis=z }
let camp_clock_mark = box -0.045 1.035 -0.045   0.045 1.215 0.045
let camp_clock_marks = translate { around { camp_clock_mark } count=12 axis=z } 0 22.05 -2.61
let camp_hand_hour = box -0.06 -0.12 -0.05   0.06 0.72 0.05
let camp_hand_min  = box -0.045 -0.12 -0.05  0.045 1.08 0.05
let camp_clock_hands = translate {
    union { rotate { camp_hand_hour } z=-0.125  rotate { camp_hand_min } z=0.19 }
} 0 22.05 -2.61
let camp_clock_boss = sphere 0 22.05 -2.61 r=0.12
let camp_clock_gilt = union { camp_clock_marks camp_clock_hands }

# ================================================================================================
# THE VANE — the thinnest matter in the project, at the highest point in it
# ================================================================================================
#
# The banner is 0.03 m thick and 1.35 m across at 34.65 m. DO NOT THICKEN IT. It is written from
# z = 0.00 to z = 0.03 rather than symmetrically about zero because the origin falls on a voxel
# BOUNDARY at metre 32 and a plate written -0.015 to +0.015 straddles it and contains no cell
# centre at all — it would vanish, silently, and look exactly like a vane that was never built.
let camp_vane_spindle = cylinder 0 33.60 0 r=0.06 h=0.90 axis=y
let camp_vane_ball    = sphere 0 33.90 0 r=0.225
let camp_vane_rod     = cylinder 0 34.2375 0 r=0.045 h=0.825 axis=y

let camp_vane_bar_x = box -0.675 34.155 0.00    0.675 34.245 0.03
let camp_vane_bar_z = box  0.00 34.155 -0.675   0.03 34.245 0.675
let camp_vane_cross = union { camp_vane_bar_x camp_vane_bar_z }

# The banner is a pennant: a plate 0.45 tall at the mast tapering to a point 1.35 out, cut by two
# sloping half spaces whose turn is atan(0.225/1.35) / tau.
let camp_vane_flag_a = translate { rotate { camp_hs_yneg } z=-0.02627 } 0 34.65 0
let camp_vane_flag_b = translate { rotate { camp_hs_ypos } z=0.02627 }  0 34.20 0
let camp_vane_flag = intersection { box -0.05 34.19 0.00   1.35 34.66 0.03
                                    camp_vane_flag_a camp_vane_flag_b }

let camp_vane_bronze = union { camp_vane_spindle camp_vane_rod camp_vane_cross camp_vane_flag }

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================
#
# The stone is grained by 16 mm and the metal and the timber are not, and that is deliberate: the
# grain is what makes a face read as carved, and on a plate 0.03 thick it is a 16 mm variation in a
# 30 mm thickness that would open holes in the banner and break the clip into pieces. So the
# displacement is applied to the masonry and nothing else, and every paint rule below still says
# `below=0.02`, which is past the 16 mm.
let camp_stone = union { camp_ground camp_carved camp_floor camp_newel
                         camp_spandrel camp_stair camp_belf_floor camp_belf_parapet
                         camp_colonnettes camp_clock_disc }
# 0.016 AND NOT THE FACILITY'S 0.012, and the number is the engine's and not a taste. Anything
# under HALF a voxel cannot move a grid-aligned surface anywhere along it; all it can do is push
# voxels back and forth across their own thresholds, which is dither, and clip_script.cpp DROPS it
# and says so in the log. Half a voxel at metre 32 is 0.015625. 0.016 is the first number above it
# that is still inside the paint rules' own below=0.02, so the grain is a real deformation and
# every rule below still reaches the voxels it made.
let camp_stone_grained = displace { camp_stone grain_fine } amount=0.016

let camp_metal = union { camp_roof_skin camp_louvres camp_bell camp_clapper
                         camp_bell_straps camp_lanterns camp_clock_bezel camp_clock_gilt
                         camp_vane_bronze camp_vane_ball }
let camp_timber = union { camp_roof_oak camp_headstock camp_bell_beam }

let camp_all_raw   = union { camp_stone camp_metal camp_timber }
let part_campanile = union { camp_stone_grained camp_metal camp_timber }

# ================================================================================================
# THE HEAD-HEIGHT PROBE — the measurement this part exists to make
# ================================================================================================
#
#     ./build/clipcheck clips/estate/campanile.clip --metre 32 --part campanile_probe_head
#
# must report an EMPTY clip. Not part of the building; nothing below is unioned into it.
#
# WHAT THE PROBE IS PULLED IN BY, and why it has to be. Over a tread it is the WALKING WIDTH and
# not the tread: 0.045 off the wall, 0.045 off the newel, and 0.15 of a tread off each riser. The
# riser in front of you is a wall, not a low ceiling, and a probe whose face lay exactly on the
# riser line would report the next tread up as a head-height failure the whole length of the stair
# — which is stair.clip's own warning about rotated probe boxes, said in cylindrical coordinates.
# 0.15 of a tread is 0.048 m at the walking line, which is a voxel and a half at metre 32.
#
# WHAT IT CANNOT SEE, so that nobody trusts it further than it goes: it tests THIS part against
# ITSELF, at the resolution it is run at, and it knows nothing about anything another clip may one
# day stand beside this tower.

# Over a tread: 2.10 m up from the tread top, between the wall and the newel, inside the going.
let camp_ph_sec = intersection { rotate { camp_hs_zneg } y=0.00454545
                                 rotate { camp_hs_zpos } y=0.02575758 }
let camp_ph_ring = difference { box -1.98 0.18 -1.98   1.98 2.28 1.98
                                cylinder 0 12 0 r=0.78 h=30 axis=y }
let camp_ph_01 = intersection { camp_ph_ring camp_ph_sec }
let camp_ph_02 = union { camp_ph_01 translate { rotate { camp_ph_01 } y=0.03030303 } 0 0.18 0 }
let camp_ph_04 = union { camp_ph_02 translate { rotate { camp_ph_02 } y=0.06060606 } 0 0.36 0 }
let camp_ph_08 = union { camp_ph_04 translate { rotate { camp_ph_04 } y=0.12121212 } 0 0.72 0 }
let camp_ph_16 = union { camp_ph_08 translate { rotate { camp_ph_08 } y=0.24242424 } 0 1.44 0 }
let camp_ph_32 = union { camp_ph_16 translate { rotate { camp_ph_16 } y=0.48484848 } 0 2.88 0 }
let camp_ph_33 = union { camp_ph_32 translate { rotate { camp_ph_01 } y=0.96969697 } 0 5.76 0 }
let camp_ph_66  = union { camp_ph_33 translate { camp_ph_33 } 0 5.94 0 }
let camp_ph_132 = union { camp_ph_66 translate { camp_ph_66 } 0 11.88 0 }
let camp_ph_134 = union { camp_ph_132
                          translate { camp_ph_01 } 0 23.76 0
                          translate { rotate { camp_ph_01 } y=0.03030303 } 0 23.94 0 }
let camp_probe_rake = rotate { camp_ph_134 } y=-0.06060606

# Over the ground floor: 2.10 m up from y = 0, everywhere a person can stand — which is the well
# less the PLAN of the two things that rise off the floor, the newel and the spandrel. A plan and
# not the shapes, for stair.clip's reason: you walk ROUND a solid, so a solid is not a low ceiling,
# but a tread two metres up over the open floor IS one and must stay in.
# THE CUTTER IS THE SECTOR GROWN BY 0.04, AND THE PROBE FOUND OUT WHY. The treads carry
# `round by=0.01`, which GROWS them: a tread is 0.01 proud of the sector column that is supposed to
# stand under it, on both radial faces and all the way up. Cut with the bare column, the probe kept
# a rind one voxel thick along thirteen sector joints and reported 790 voxels of "low ceiling" that
# were nothing but the round. 0.04 is the round plus half a voxel at metre 32 (0.0156) and a
# little; `offset` with a NEGATIVE value grows, which is stair.clip's own sign note again.
#
# The newel's cutter is grown the same way, from the newel's 0.675 to 0.78: the treads run THROUGH
# the newel and their inner ends are buried in it, so the cutter has to clear the newel's own round
# as well as the treads'.
let camp_pf_col = offset { camp_sp_col } by=-0.04
let camp_pf_stand = union { cylinder 0 12 0 r=0.78 h=30 axis=y
                            rotate { camp_pf_col } y=-0.06060606
                            rotate { camp_pf_col } y=-0.03030303
                            camp_pf_col }
let camp_pf_stand_b = union { rotate { camp_pf_col } y=0.03030303
                              rotate { camp_pf_col } y=0.06060606
                              rotate { camp_pf_col } y=0.09090909
                              rotate { camp_pf_col } y=0.12121212 }
let camp_pf_stand_c = union { rotate { camp_pf_col } y=0.15151515
                              rotate { camp_pf_col } y=0.18181818
                              rotate { camp_pf_col } y=0.21212121
                              rotate { camp_pf_col } y=0.24242424 }
let camp_pf_stand_d = union { rotate { camp_pf_col } y=0.27272727
                              rotate { camp_pf_col } y=0.30303030 }
let camp_probe_floor = difference { box -1.98 0.00 -1.98   1.98 2.10 1.98
                                    camp_pf_stand camp_pf_stand_b
                                    camp_pf_stand_c camp_pf_stand_d }

# Over the belfry floor, and through the door.
let camp_probe_belf = box -1.98 24.30 -1.98   1.98 26.40 -0.28
# 0.90 wide and not 1.35. The head is a semicircle of radius 0.675, so the clear width AT 2.40 m
# is 1.12 m and not the doorway's full 1.35 — a probe the whole width of the opening would report
# the arch itself, which is not a thing anybody walks into.
let camp_probe_door = box -3.60 0.00 -0.45   -1.90 2.40 0.45

let camp_probe_all = union { camp_probe_rake camp_probe_floor
                             camp_probe_belf camp_probe_door }
# AGAINST `camp_all_raw` AND NOT AGAINST `part_campanile`, which is the same choice stair.clip
# makes: the grain displaces the masonry outward by up to 12 mm at the very end, and a probe whose
# floor lies exactly on a tread top would then report every tread in the tower. The clearances this
# building holds are 0.15 m and 0.05 m — ten times the grain and four times it — so the grain is
# inside the margin, and measuring the geometry is the honest measurement.
let campanile_probe_head = intersection { camp_all_raw camp_probe_all }

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT — every rule keyed on one of this file's own shapes, every one at below=0.02
# ================================================================================================
#
# A tower is not one stone: sandstone ashlar for the mass because it is the softest thing that will
# stand out of doors and it is what weathers; granite where it meets the ground and where a foot
# lands; limestone for everything DRESSED — the quoins, the strips, the corbel tables, the cornice
# and the belfry — because dressed stone in a building of this date is a different bed from the
# walling and reads a shade paler; marble for the two slender things, the colonnettes and the clock
# face. Then the metals, which are the whole reason the crown of this tower is worth walking to:
# bronze, gilt, copper and iron, four of them within four metres of each other at 30 m.
# THE ORDER IS THE RULE, because a later rule wins where two overlap and every one of these
# overlaps its neighbour by construction — a quoin runs down through the plinth, the plinth stands
# in the gravel. Sandstone first as the coat everything else is cut out of, then the ground, then
# the dressed stone, then the granite, so the plinth stays granite where the quoin crosses it.
# The base coat is the SHELL and the parapet, not the whole of `camp_stone`: everything else made
# of stone here — the ground, the floor, the newel, the spandrel, the treads, the belfry floor, the
# colonnettes, the clock face — has a rule of its own below, and asking the sampler to evaluate the
# entire building a second time to paint what four cheaper rules already cover is a cost with
# nothing on the other side of it.
let camp_ashlar = union { camp_carved camp_belf_parapet }

# ================================================================================================
# THE THREE WEATHERING ZONES — and this is a MEASURED shape, not a drawn one
# ================================================================================================
#
# Weathering was 10.7 times the whole cost of this clip, and the control arm is what says so rather
# than a guess: at metre 8, with the three `weather` lines commented out, 13.8 seconds of CPU; with
# them, 147.9. Two things were wrong with the way they were scoped, and both are fixed here.
#
# 1. THE SCOPE WAS `camp_carved`, WHICH IS THE WHOLE MASONRY — including the four faces of the
#    stair well, 24 m tall, which is more surface than the outside of the tower and is a place
#    weather has never been. `camp_weather_skin` is the masonry caught in a hollow box: everything
#    from 0.40 inside the outer face outward, nothing within 2.30 of the axis. The well's walls,
#    the belfry's inner faces and the whole of the stair are outside every weather rule now, which
#    is both cheaper and correct — stair.clip's "inside stone stays clean", one storey up.
#
# 2. THE TWO BIG SCOPES WERE THE SAME SHAPE, SO THEY NESTED. clip_script.cpp replaces the solid
#    with a displacement of it for each request in turn, and takes the NEXT request's occlusion and
#    curvature over that. Two requests over one scope is therefore not twice the cost, it is the
#    second one's six-sample curvature multiplied by the first one's — and `overgrown` samples an
#    OCCLUSION, which is dearer still. So the order is now cracks, desert, overgrown:
#    THE EXPENSIVE MASK GOES FIRST, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE IT SHOULD BE, and
#    the control arm is what settled it. Ordered cracks-desert-overgrown — cheap first — metre 8
#    cost 308.8 seconds of CPU against 13.8 with the three lines commented out: TWENTY-TWO TIMES,
#    and worse than the 147.9 the naive scoping had cost. The reason is that a request does not pay
#    for its own mask once, it pays for every mask BENEATH it once per sample it takes: `cracks`
#    asks a `cell_edge`, and putting it at the bottom made every one of `desert`'s six curvature
#    samples and every one of `overgrown`'s occlusion samples pay for a Worley cell lookup.
#    So the order is by how DEAR a request's own mask is, dearest at the bottom:
#      `overgrown` asks an OCCLUSION, the dearest thing here, so it goes first and on the SMALLEST
#                  scope, where its mask is nought almost everywhere and `Op::Multiply` stops at
#                  the first nought factor.
#      `desert`    asks one curvature — six samples of a solid whose only extra term is a mask that
#                  is already nought outside the plinth.
#      `cracks`    asks the field NOTHING. Its mask is `cell_edge` and `fbm`, so it is free to sit
#                  on top, where nothing above it ever evaluates it more than once.
#
# The damp zone is the two plinth steps and a slab lying against the NORTH face — the face that
# never sees the sun — both caught in the same skin so the moss is on the outside only.
let camp_skin_shell = difference { box -3.70 -0.65 -3.70   3.70 29.30 3.70
                                   box -2.30 -0.70 -2.30   2.30 29.40 2.30 }
let camp_weather_skin = intersection { camp_masonry camp_skin_shell }
let camp_damp_north = box -2.80 0.30 2.55   2.80 24.30 2.90
let camp_damp_raw = union { camp_plinth_1 camp_plinth_2 camp_damp_north }
let camp_damp = intersection { camp_damp_raw camp_skin_shell }
paint sandstone where=camp_ashlar below=0.02
paint lawn      where=camp_lawn   below=0.02
paint gravel    where=camp_gravel below=0.02

let camp_dressed = union { camp_quoins camp_lesenes camp_bands camp_cornice camp_belfry }
paint limestone where=camp_dressed below=0.02

let camp_granite_zone = union { camp_plinth_1 camp_plinth_2 camp_floor camp_newel camp_spandrel }
paint granite where=camp_granite_zone below=0.02

# The treads are marble and the stair beneath them granite, which is the facility's own reading of
# a stair: hard stone where it is founded, the better bed where the eye follows it up.
paint marble where=camp_stair below=0.02
let camp_marble_zone = union { camp_belf_floor camp_colonnettes camp_clock_disc }
paint marble where=camp_marble_zone below=0.02

paint copper where=camp_roof_skin below=0.02
paint oak    where=camp_timber    below=0.02
paint iron   where=camp_louvres   below=0.02

let camp_iron_zone = union { camp_clapper camp_bell_straps camp_lanterns_frame }
paint iron where=camp_iron_zone below=0.02

let camp_bronze_zone = union { camp_bell camp_vane_bronze camp_clock_bezel camp_clock_boss }
paint bronze where=camp_bronze_zone below=0.02

let camp_gilt_zone = union { camp_vane_ball camp_clock_gilt }
paint gilt where=camp_gilt_zone below=0.02

paint lamp where=camp_lanterns_glass below=0.02

# ================================================================================================
# WEATHERING — the most exposed object on the estate, and it is old
# ================================================================================================
#
# Every rule names ONE OF THIS FILE'S OWN SHAPES. Without `on=` the coats are keyed on a value
# rather than on a place and they would bleach this clip's own lawn along with the ashlar, which is
# the reason the last facility shipped with no weathering at all.
#
# THE STAIR IS LEFT ALONE ENTIRELY: it is indoors, it is the one surface in this clip whose
# dimensions are held to a tenth of a metre, and no render would tell you which way a weathering
# coat had taken a going. Inside stone stays clean, which is stair.clip's rule and its reason.
# THE CRACKS ARE 0.20 AT SCALE 0.90 AND NOT 0.07 AT 0.45, for the same reason the grain is 0.016.
# A crack's depth is 0.09 x scale x amount, so 0.07 at 0.45 is 2.8 mm — a tenth of a voxel — and it
# was dropped by the same cutoff, leaving the fissure PAINT with no fissure under it: a dark line
# drawn on a flat face. 0.20 at 0.90 is 16 mm, which is a crack.
#
# AND THERE ARE THREE REQUESTS AND NOT FIVE. Two plinth steps asked separately were two whole
# passes to say one thing, so they are one scope now; and the roof was a fifth, for a deformation
# of 2.4 mm that the half-voxel cutoff was always going to drop anyway. The scopes and the ORDER
# are argued where they are bound, above the paint, and both were decided against a control arm.
#
# THE COPPER THEREFORE CARRIES NO WEATHER RULE AT ALL, and that is the brief's own instruction
# followed to its end: `copper` in the contract is rgb 96,140,124, which is not metal, it is
# PATINA — the material is already the weathering, and a desert coat over it would only put sand on
# a green roof.
weather overgrown 0.13 scale=0.70 seed=37 on=camp_damp
weather desert    0.11 scale=0.90 seed=29 on=camp_weather_skin
weather cracks    0.20 scale=0.90 seed=31 on=camp_weather_skin

variation colour=0.030 rough=0.070 seed=29

solid part_campanile
